---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import { apexTouches, fieldDependents } from "../../data/page-answers.ts";

const words = ["no", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten"];
const reads = words[apexTouches.groups[0]?.items.length ?? 0];
const writes = words[apexTouches.groups[1]?.items.length ?? 0];
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Salesforce Dependency Analysis for Fields, Flows and Apex"
  description="Trace what depends on a Salesforce field, flow or Apex class, and what it depends on, across code, automation, layouts, sharing and analytics."
  path="/use-cases/salesforce-dependency-analysis"
  heading="Map what depends on what"
  lede="Salesforce dependencies are spread across flows, Apex, formulas, layouts, permissions and sharing rules. Trace them in both directions, from one question, with each link named."
  crumb="Dependency analysis"
  audience="architects and developers"
  short="Ask your AI assistant “what depends on the Status field on Project?” or “what does PaymentService read and write?”. sf-intelligence follows the links in your org’s metadata in either direction and lists each one with how it was found: stated in the metadata, read from parsed source, or matched by a pattern you should double-check."
  answers={[fieldDependents, apexTouches]}
  answerIntro="Two traces on Verdant, the demo org: everything that depends on one field, and everything one Apex class touches."
  asks={[
    "What depends on the Status field on Project?",
    "What does the PaymentService class read and write?",
    "What calls this Apex method?",
    "What does the Installation On Complete flow update?",
    "What of mine depends on the managed package I want to uninstall?",
  ]}
  checks={[
    "<strong>Fields and objects</strong>: formulas, roll-ups, flows, validation rules, layouts, sharing rules",
    "<strong>Apex</strong>: the fields a class reads and writes, and who calls it, from parsed source",
    "<strong>Flows</strong>: what starts them, what they read and what they change",
    "<strong>Lightning and Aura components</strong> that call Apex, when your refresh pulled them",
    "<strong>Packages</strong>: your components that reference a namespace",
  ]}
  limits={[
    "Calls made by a name built at run time, or through reflection",
    "Code inside managed packages",
    "Integrations calling in from outside Salesforce",
    "Kinds of metadata your refresh didn’t pull. Each answer lists them",
  ]}
  tools={["get_impact", "get_edges", "call_graph", "find_component_usages", "downstream_effects", "package_impact"]}
  related={[
    { href: "/use-cases/where-is-a-field-used", label: "Where is a field used?", note: "use case" },
    { href: "/use-cases/impact-analysis", label: "Impact analysis", note: "with a verdict" },
    { href: "/use-cases/explain-a-salesforce-flow", label: "Explain a flow", note: "use case" },
    { href: "/blog/deterministic-reasoning-model", label: "How sf-intelligence reasons about structure", note: "for developers" },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "What do “stated”, “parsed” and “pattern match” mean?",
      a: "They say how a link was found. Stated means Salesforce’s metadata names the dependency directly. Parsed means it was read from Apex, flow or formula source. A pattern match is a weaker text match, so check it before you act on it.",
    },
    {
      q: "Why does access show up separately?",
      a: "A profile that can see a field doesn’t depend on it, and won’t break if it changes. Permission grants are listed on their own so they don’t inflate the list of things to fix.",
    },
    {
      q: "Can it show a diagram?",
      a: "Answers can include a Mermaid diagram of the dependency graph, which assistants such as Claude can render.",
    },
  ]}
  cta={{ question: fieldDependents.question, related: { href: "/use-cases/impact-analysis", label: "Turn the trace into a verdict" } }}
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <h2>Two directions</h2>
  <p><strong>What depends on this?</strong> tells you what to check before you change something. In the demo org, the Project Status field feeds a trigger handler, a batch class, two flows, a validation rule, a formula, a layout, a sharing rule and an analytics dataset. <strong>What does this depend on?</strong> tells you what a component needs. PaymentService reads {reads} Invoice fields and writes {writes}, which is why changing Invoice Status needs a look at that class first.</p>
</UseCaseLayout>
